Dialogue: 0,0:00:01.80,0:00:06.37,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}A basic logistic regression separates\Nadults with and without diabetes
Dialogue: 0,0:00:06.37,0:00:08.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}at an AUC of 0.961.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.85,0:00:13.30,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}From survey data alone,\Nthat is suspiciously high.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:13.30,0:00:16.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I'm [Name];\NI led governance and integration.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:16.70,0:00:19.55,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] led data quality and preparation,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:19.55,0:00:24.41,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] exploration, labels and bias,\Nand [Name] modelling and conclusions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:24.41,0:00:28.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We present in that order,\Nand the 0.961 runs through the whole talk.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.30,0:00:34.88,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our question: who gets labelled diabetic,\Nand what do preparation decisions change?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.88,0:00:40.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The design: one L2 logistic regression\Non three preparations of the same data,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.28,0:00:42.52,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}naive, minimal and systematic,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:42.52,0:00:45.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}scored on all adults\Nand on adults not yet diagnosed.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:46.95,0:00:50.23,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our research questions:\Nwhat does the label measure?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.23,0:00:52.35,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Is the high AUC leakage?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.35,0:00:54.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Does systematic preparation pay?
Dialogue: 0,0:00:55.30,0:00:59.85,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The data: NHANES 2021 to 2023,\Nthe CDC's national health survey.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.85,0:01:04.41,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our cohort is 5,730 non-pregnant adults\Nwith an HbA1c result.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:04.41,0:01:07.02,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The label is positive\Nfor a self-reported diagnosis,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:07.02,0:01:09.73,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}or HbA1c of 6.5 percent or more.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:09.73,0:01:12.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Survey-weighted,\N14.0 percent are positive.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:14.30,0:01:18.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Governance came first.\NThe NCHS data use agreement forbids
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.20,0:01:21.66,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}re-identification and linkage,\Nso we did neither.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:21.66,0:01:27.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We kept 78 of 175 columns,\Nverified every raw file by hash,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.20,0:01:31.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and locked our hypotheses by hash\Nbefore running any model.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.30,0:01:36.23,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}To check our survey-weighting code,\Nwe re-ran NCHS Data Brief 516:
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.23,0:01:40.14,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}total prevalence 15.79 percent\Nagainst the published 15.8,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:40.14,0:01:42.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and all 27 published\Nestimates match closely.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:44.30,0:01:46.33,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Correct numbers can still mislead.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:46.33,0:01:47.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}In [Name]'s analysis,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.90,0:01:52.57,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}insured adults show 5.3 points\Nmore diabetes than the uninsured.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.57,0:01:56.32,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}That is confounding by age:\Nthe insured are much older.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:56.32,0:01:57.49,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Age-standardised,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:57.49,0:02:01.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the gap falls to 0.7 points,\Nand its interval includes zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.30,0:02:09.48,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Most unexpected for me: of six hypotheses\Nlocked before we saw any related data,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.48,0:02:11.17,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}only three were supported.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:12.42,0:02:15.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Now [Name] asks whether\Nthe data deserve the same trust.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:17.50,0:02:20.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I'm [Name];\NI led data quality and preparation.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:20.90,0:02:22.74,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I profiled before cleaning.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:22.74,0:02:29.01,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}NHANES stores 'refused' and 'don't know'\Nas numeric codes such as 7, 9, 77 and 99.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:29.01,0:02:32.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The common shortcut sets those values\Nto missing in every column.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:32.65,0:02:38.52,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}That catches 57 genuine codes,\Nand destroys 1,496 valid measurements,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:38.52,0:02:42.70,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}including HbA1c\Nvalues of exactly 7.0 percent.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:44.30,0:02:48.48,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Each 7.0 is above the 6.5 threshold:\Na diabetes case.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:48.48,0:02:53.44,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The shortcut erased 29 of 721\NHbA1c-based positives.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:54.69,0:02:56.15,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Only six changed label;
Dialogue: 0,0:02:56.15,0:03:00.01,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the rest had also self-reported,\Nso the either-or label hid the damage.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.01,0:03:01.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}No summary statistic shows this.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:02.30,0:03:03.53,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Second, outliers.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:03.53,0:03:08.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The 1.5 times IQR rule\Nflags 542 HbA1c values.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:09.33,0:03:11.69,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Its upper fence lies above\Nthe diagnostic threshold,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:11.69,0:03:17.14,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so the 519 high values are diabetes\Nby definition: the disease, not errors.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:17.14,0:03:18.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We reviewed and kept them all.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:20.50,0:03:23.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name]'s exploration\Nfound redundancy and skew.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:23.20,0:03:26.16,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Weight and BMI show\Nsevere multicollinearity:
Dialogue: 0,0:03:26.16,0:03:30.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}variance inflation factors\Nof 95.7 and 97.6.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:31.25,0:03:35.24,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Fasting triglycerides are strongly\Nright-skewed: skewness 6.62.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:36.29,0:03:38.04,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Both matter for a linear model,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:38.04,0:03:41.91,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so the systematic preparation drops weight\Nand applies a Yeo-Johnson transform,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:41.91,0:03:43.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}which brings the skewness close to zero.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.30,0:03:47.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Each finding became a switchable step,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:47.10,0:03:52.05,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}fourteen in all, each tied\Nto its evidence, and none reads the label.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.10,0:03:56.42,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}From them we built three\Nversions of the data.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.42,0:03:58.67,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Naive keeps every column.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:58.67,0:04:04.68,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Minimal adds one rule: drop items\Nthat exist only after a diagnosis,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.68,0:04:06.14,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}such as treatment.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:06.14,0:04:08.68,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Systematic adds my other steps,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:08.68,0:04:12.79,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}from per-variable recoding\Nto transforms and class weights.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.79,0:04:16.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}All three feed the same model\Non the same folds.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.30,0:04:18.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Most unexpected for me:
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.97,0:04:23.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}one routine cleaning line erased\N29 diabetic HbA1c results,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:23.60,0:04:24.92,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and the label hid it.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.17,0:04:28.72,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}But cleaning cannot fix the label itself.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:28.72,0:04:29.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] shows why.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:31.50,0:04:34.88,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I'm [Name];\NI led exploration, labels and bias.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:34.88,0:04:39.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}First, a fallacy check:\Nwe scanned 224 subgroup comparisons
Dialogue: 0,0:04:39.60,0:04:42.24,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}for Simpson's paradox,\Nand six reversed sign.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:43.49,0:04:47.63,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}A causal diagram suggests why:\NBMI lies between race and diabetes,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.63,0:04:50.56,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so adjusting\Nfor it distorts the comparison.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:50.56,0:04:52.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}We report this as exploratory.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.30,0:04:55.62,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Research question one: the label.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.62,0:04:58.99,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our label combines two sources,\Nand they agree substantially
Dialogue: 0,0:04:58.99,0:05:02.67,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}but far from perfectly:\NCohen's kappa is 0.70.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:03.92,0:05:10.08,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Of 819 self-reported diabetics,\N250 test below 6.5 percent,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:10.08,0:05:12.45,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and two thirds of those\Ntake insulin or pills.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:13.50,0:05:16.27,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Treatment lowers\Nthe very marker we label by,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.27,0:05:19.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so a blood-test-only label\Nwould call treated patients healthy.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:20.30,0:05:21.75,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The other direction:
Dialogue: 0,0:05:21.75,0:05:27.07,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}152 adults have HbA1c of 6.5 or more\Nbut no diagnosis.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:28.32,0:05:30.58,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Among adults with high HbA1c,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:30.58,0:05:35.14,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the uninsured are 23.2 percentage points\Nmore often undiagnosed,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:35.14,0:05:39.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}interval 6.2 to 40.1,\Nan association from a small subgroup.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:40.90,0:05:43.60,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}So the label also records access to care.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.60,0:05:47.16,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}That is why we also evaluate\Non adults not yet diagnosed:
Dialogue: 0,0:05:47.16,0:05:48.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the people screening is for.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.50,0:05:53.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The leakage question,\Nfrom [Name]'s experiments.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:53.28,0:05:56.65,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Naive preparation scores an AUC of 0.961.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:56.65,0:06:00.48,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Minimal preparation removes\Nonly the post-diagnosis items:
Dialogue: 0,0:06:00.48,0:06:03.63,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}treatment, diagnosis history\Nand prior glucose testing.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:03.63,0:06:08.84,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Same model, same folds: the AUC falls\Nto 0.828, a paired drop of 0.131.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:10.59,0:06:12.56,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}A deterioration we wanted:
Dialogue: 0,0:06:12.56,0:06:15.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the 0.961 was the label leaking back in,\Nnot skill.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:16.30,0:06:19.87,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name]'s shallow decision tree\Nshows the mechanism.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.87,0:06:24.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Its root is a treatment item; one branch\Nthen splits on age at diagnosis.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:24.97,0:06:27.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}But 86 percent were never asked
Dialogue: 0,0:06:27.90,0:06:32.83,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}that question, and median imputation\Nfilled every blank with 50.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:34.08,0:06:37.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}So 'age 50' became a code\Nfor 'never diagnosed'.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:38.30,0:06:39.85,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Most unexpected for me:
Dialogue: 0,0:06:39.85,0:06:44.63,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}at one shared threshold, the model flags\N70 of every 100 diagnosed diabetics,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:44.63,0:06:47.82,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}but only 52 of 100 undiagnosed ones.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.07,0:06:50.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name] takes the last question.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:52.50,0:06:55.29,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}I'm [Name];\NI led modelling and conclusions.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:55.29,0:06:58.26,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Last question:\Ndoes systematic preparation pay?
Dialogue: 0,0:06:58.26,0:07:00.80,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Same model, minimal against systematic:
Dialogue: 0,0:07:00.80,0:07:05.99,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}AUC rises by 0.008,\Ninterval 0.000 to 0.016.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:07.44,0:07:11.45,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Our smallest effect of interest,\Nfixed in advance, was 0.01.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:11.45,0:07:14.21,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The interval covers both no gain\Nand a worthwhile one,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:14.21,0:07:18.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so the result is inconclusive:\Nunderpowered, not evidence of no effect.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:19.30,0:07:20.51,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}One step did harm.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:20.51,0:07:25.04,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}From minimal to systematic,\Nthe Brier score worsens by 0.074.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:26.29,0:07:28.00,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The cause is [Name]'s class weights:
Dialogue: 0,0:07:28.00,0:07:32.23,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}the systematic model predicts\Nan average risk of 41 percent
Dialogue: 0,0:07:32.23,0:07:34.85,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}where the observed rate is 17.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:34.85,0:07:37.87,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Removing only the class weights\Nrestores calibration
Dialogue: 0,0:07:37.87,0:07:41.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and leaves AUC 0.007 above minimal.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:43.50,0:07:47.95,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Who does the score serve? On all adults,\Nthe systematic model scores 0.836;
Dialogue: 0,0:07:47.95,0:07:50.38,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}on adults not yet diagnosed, 0.781.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:51.83,0:07:54.93,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}84 percent of positives\Nwere already diagnosed,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:54.93,0:07:58.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}so the all-adults AUC mostly\Nmeasures recognition: spectrum bias.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:59.30,0:08:03.03,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Next, drop two items\Nthat mark earlier contact with a doctor.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:03.03,0:08:06.56,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}On all adults, AUC falls by 0.016.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:06.56,0:08:08.40,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}On adults not yet diagnosed,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:08.40,0:08:13.95,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}it rises by 0.019,\Ninterval 0.004 to 0.034:
Dialogue: 0,0:08:13.95,0:08:17.28,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}consistent with recognition\Nand screening pulling apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:18.53,0:08:22.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}But the lower end lies inside our margin,\Nso it is inconclusive.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:23.30,0:08:27.20,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}An internal hold-out of 1,146 adults,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:27.20,0:08:31.68,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}scored once, repeats the leakage drop\Nand the inconclusive preparation result.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:31.68,0:08:32.98,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}So, three answers.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:32.98,0:08:35.19,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The label records care as well as disease.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:36.24,0:08:38.42,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The 0.961 was leakage, not skill.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:39.47,0:08:44.26,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Systematic preparation barely moved AUC;\Nits value was valid data kept
Dialogue: 0,0:08:44.26,0:08:47.10,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and an honest test\Non adults not yet diagnosed.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:48.30,0:08:50.03,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Most unexpected for me:
Dialogue: 0,0:08:50.03,0:08:53.90,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}leakage moved the score far more\Nthan careful preparation did.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:55.30,0:08:56.99,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Four roles, one audit:
Dialogue: 0,0:08:56.99,0:09:01.97,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}[Name]'s governance, [Name]'s preparation,\N[Name]'s labels and bias, my models.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:01.97,0:09:04.64,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Limits: one survey cycle, low response,
Dialogue: 0,0:09:04.64,0:09:08.66,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}and only 152 undiagnosed positives;
Dialogue: 0,0:09:08.66,0:09:11.68,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}this is a data audit,\Nnot a screening tool.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:11.68,0:09:14.88,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Generative AI drafted\Nmuch of our code and text;
Dialogue: 0,0:09:14.88,0:09:18.13,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}we reviewed it\Nand recomputed every number.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:19.38,0:09:23.59,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}The score is not the finding;\Nwhat it reveals about the data is.
Dialogue: 0,0:09:23.59,0:09:24.59,Caption,,0,0,0,,{\fad(100,100)}Thank you.
